Title: The Little Red Frog

The little frog lived in Froggy Courtyard, where all the frogs wore green clothes. The little frog thought this outfit was quite ordinary—not beautiful at all. Then she saw the Canna flowers in the yard. Their petals were bright red, like flames. How beautiful! The little frog found her red watercolor paint and painted it all over her body, leaving only her shiny eyes untouched.

A little swallow flew overhead and exclaimed in surprise, "Look! There's a red frog sitting on a lotus leaf. How lovely!" Filled with joy at the swallow's praise, the little frog called out loudly, "I'm a one-of-a-kind red frog!"

The little frog's tummy rumbled, so she went out to catch grasshoppers. But the grasshoppers had keen eyes. Spotting the red frog approaching, one went "boing" and disappeared into the grass. "One grasshopper got away. I'll go after a mole cricket instead!" The little frog crept forward quietly. But that mischievous mole cricket, too, saw the red frog coming and silently burrowed into the mud. The little frog was puzzled and murmured, "What's going on? These bugs have suddenly become so clever?" After busying herself for a while, she hadn't caught a single insect. "Oh dear, what bad luck today!"

Suddenly, she heard a rustling sound—*slap, slap, slap*. The little frog turned around and froze. "Oh no! A big green snake is coming!" The snake held its head high and flicked its tongue, ready to swallow the little frog. Terrified, the little frog ran as fast as she could. Ahead, she spotted a pond. "Splash!" She jumped right in.

Swimming and swimming in the pond, the red paint washed off her body, and she returned to her original green clothes. The little frog finally understood—it was all her own fault. Wearing that bright red outfit had made her an easy target. So conspicuous!

At that moment, she heard another *splash*. Mother Frog had jumped into the pond and swam over. "My child, what happened to you?" Mother Frog asked. The little frog replied, "Mother, the green clothes you gave me are wonderful. They match the grass perfectly—it's the best kind of camouflage."

Mother Frog and the little frog croaked with happiness together.

Knowledge

Frogs are lovely little animals that are helpful to humans. On summer nights, they sing "ribbit, ribbit" all night long. Frogs have many special features:

Frogs blink their eyes when they swallow food;
On each side of a frog's head, there is a slightly bulging little patch—that's its eardrum. Frogs hear sounds through it;
Frogs lay eggs. The eggs hatch into tadpoles, and tadpoles eventually grow up to become frogs;
A frog's eyes are very special. When tiny animals of all shapes fly or move quickly past, the frog can instantly recognize the insects it loves to eat most—like flies and moths—while completely ignoring anything else that moves or stays still;
Frogs are expert bug catchers. When a mosquito buzzes by, the frog leaps upward in a flash, extends its tongue, and snaps it back. The mosquito is gone, swallowed whole. After its meal, the frog settles right back into position, waiting patiently for the next pest to arrive;
Frogs are cold-blooded animals, meaning their body temperature changes along with the outside temperature.
